86486292 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "83bce5eb-d032-50f1-80c7-b8e437dbc204" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186486293 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186485761 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186486293 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186485857 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186486293 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186485883 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186486293 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186485897 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186486293 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186485918 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186486293 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186486293 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186486293 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186485761 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186486293 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "8915730a-0d54-5b73-a211-633a544404bc" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186486342 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186485771 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186486342 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186485813 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186486342 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186485919 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186486342 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186486032 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186486342 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186486199 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186486342 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186486342 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186486342 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186485919 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186486342 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "f5c09547-1e15-5c60-92ba-f36758f345fa" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186486361 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186486239 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186486361 #edb/ref 1069 "q-78207" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186486361 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186486361 #edb/ref 1071 "Find the equation of the osculating plane (in parametric form) for the curve $r(t)$ at the point where $t=0$, given that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(t)=t\\,i+{t}^{2}\\,j+{t}^{3}\\,k,\\,T(0)=i,\\,N(0)=j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that $T$ and $N$ denote the unit tangent and principal normal, respectively." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186486361 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "1cbb1ea4-6349-500a-851f-5c768482f036" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186486361 #edb/ref 1074 "The osculating plane passes through the point that is at the tip of the vector $r(0).$ The vectors $T(0)$ and $N(0)$ are parallel to this plane. Therefore, the equation of the plane (in parametric form) can be written as\n\n$$\nR(s,u)=r(0)+s\\,T(0)+u\\,N(0),\\,s,u\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).\n$$\n\n \n\nWe have that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(0) & =0\\,i+{0}^{2}\\,j+{0}^{3}\\,k=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, the equation of the osculating plane at the point $(0,0,0)$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}R(s,u) & =r(0)+s\\,T(0)+u\\,N(0) \\\\  & =0+s(i)+u(j) \\\\  & =s\\,i+u\\,j.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186486378 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186486275 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186486378 #edb/ref 1069 "q-79233" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186486378 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186486378 #edb/ref 1071 "Find the shortest distance between the origin and the osculating plane for the curve $r(t)$ at the point where $t=-\\pi ,$ given that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(t) & =⟨{sin}^{3}⁡t,{cos}^{3}⁡t,\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t⟩ \\\\ T(-\\pi ) & =\\frac{1}{5}⟨0,3,-4⟩ \\\\ N(-\\pi ) & =⟨-1,0,0⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that $T$ and $N$ denote the unit tangent and principal normal, respectively." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186486378 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "47ce2283-c10d-5cc5-8c7c-2556a8407b56" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186486378 #edb/ref 1074 "First, notice that  the position vector of the point where $t=-\\pi $ is\n\n$$\np=r(-\\pi )=⟨{sin}^{3}⁡(-\\pi ),{cos}^{3}⁡(-\\pi ),\\operatorname{cos}⁡(-2\\pi )⟩=⟨0,-1,1⟩.\n$$\n\nNow, we need to find a vector that's perpendicular to the osculating plane. To do that, we use the cross product of the unit tangent $T(-\\pi )$ and the principal normal $N(-\\pi ):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T(-\\pi )\\times N(-\\pi ) & =\\frac{1}{5}⟨0,3,-4⟩\\times ⟨-1,0,0⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}\\left|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ 0 & 3 & -4 \\\\ -1 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}\\right| \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}[\\text{ }\\left|\\begin{aligned}3 & -4 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}\\right|\\,i-\\left|\\begin{aligned}0 & -4 \\\\ -1 & 0\\end{aligned}\\right|\\,j+\\left|\\begin{aligned}0 & 3 \\\\ -1 & 0\\end{aligned}\\right|\\,k\\text{ }] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}⟨0,4,3⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the shortest distance between our plane and the origin is given by\n\n$$\nd=\\frac{|(p-0)\\cdot n|}{‖n‖}=\\frac{|p\\cdot n|}{‖n‖}=|p\\cdot n|,\n$$\n\nsince $‖n‖=1.$ Therefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}d & =|p\\cdot n| \\\\  & =|⟨0,-1,1⟩\\cdot \\frac{1}{5}⟨0,4,3⟩| \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}|\\,0-4+3\\,| \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186486416 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186486252 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186486416 #edb/ref 1069 "q-79234" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186486416 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186486416 #edb/ref 1071 "Find the shortest distance between the origin and the osculating plane for the curve $r(t)$ at the point where $t=0$, given that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(t) & =({t}^{2}+\\sqrt{2})\\,i+(\\sqrt{2}-{t}^{2})\\,j+2t\\,k \\\\ T(0) & =k \\\\ N(0) & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}(i-\\,j)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that $T$ and $N$ denote the unit tangent and principal normal, respectively." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186486416 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "955e768a-bc00-543e-ac79-17f131f88a54" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186486416 #edb/ref 1074 "First, notice that the position vector of the point where $t=0$ is\n\n$$\np=r(0)=(0+\\sqrt{2})\\,i+(\\sqrt{2}-0)\\,j+0\\,k=⟨\\sqrt{2},\\sqrt{2},0⟩.\n$$\n\nNow, we need to find a vector that's perpendicular to the osculating plane. To do that, we use the cross product of the unit tangent $T(0)$ and the principal normal $N(0):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}n & =T(0)\\times N(0) \\\\  & =k\\times (\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}(i-\\,j)) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\left|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 1 & -1 & 0\\end{aligned}\\right| \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}[\\text{ }\\left|\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 0\\end{aligned}\\right|\\,i-\\left|\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{aligned}\\right|\\,j+\\left|\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 \\\\ 1 & -1\\end{aligned}\\right|\\,k\\text{ }] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}⟨1,1,0⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the shortest distance between our plane and the origin is given by\n\n$$\nd=\\frac{|(p-0)\\cdot n|}{‖n‖}=\\frac{|p\\cdot n|}{‖n‖}=|p\\cdot n|,\n$$\n\nsince $‖n‖=1.$ Therefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}d & =|p\\cdot n| \\\\  & =|⟨\\sqrt{2},\\sqrt{2},0⟩\\cdot \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}⟨1,1,0⟩| \\\\  & =|1+1| \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186486418 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186486290 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186486418 #edb/ref 1069 "q-127314" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186486418 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186486418 #edb/ref 1071 "Find the shortest distance between the origin and the osculating plane for the curve $r(t)$ at the point $P(1,-1,5)$ where $t=0,$ given that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T(0)=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{14}}⟨-2,-1,3⟩,\\,N(0)=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}⟨1,1,1⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that $T$ and $N$ denote the unit tangent and principal normal, respectively." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186486418 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "982718cb-04c5-5f70-b128-45fa2db5cdc7" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186486418 #edb/ref 1074 "First, notice that the position vector of the point $P(1,-1,5)$ is $p=i-j+5\\,k.$\n\nWe need to find a vector that's perpendicular to the osculating plane. To do that, we use the cross product of the unit tangent $T(0)$ and the principal normal $N(0):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}n & =T(0)\\times N(0) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{14}}⟨-2,-1,3⟩\\times \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}⟨1,1,1⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{42}}\\,\\left|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ -2 & -1 & 3 \\\\ 1 & 1 & 1\\end{aligned}\\right| \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{42}}[\\left|\\begin{aligned}-1 & 3 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}\\right|\\,i-\\left|\\begin{aligned}-2 & 3 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}\\right|\\,j+\\left|\\begin{aligned}-2 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}\\right|\\,k] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{42}}(-4\\,i+5\\,j-\\,k)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the shortest distance between our plane and the origin is given by\n\n$$\nd=\\frac{|(p-0)\\cdot n|}{‖n‖}=\\frac{|p\\cdot n|}{‖n‖}=|p\\cdot n|,\n$$\n\nsince $‖n‖=1.$ Therefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}d & =|p\\cdot n| \\\\  & =|(i-j+5\\,k)\\cdot \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{42}}(-4\\,i+5\\,j-\\,k)| \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{42}}|-4-5-5| \\\\  & =\\frac{|-14|}{\\sqrt{42}} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{42}}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186486424 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186486286 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186486424 #edb/ref 1069 "q-79231" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186486424 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186486424 #edb/ref 1071 "Find the shortest distance between the origin and the osculating plane for the curve $r(t)$ at the point $P(4,0,4)$ where $t=-2$, given that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T(-2) & =\\frac{1}{2}⟨-1,\\,\\sqrt{2},\\,1⟩ \\\\ N(-2) & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}⟨1,\\,0,\\,1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that $T$ and $N$ denote the unit tangent and principal normal, respectively." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186486424 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "ad7e0597-ae11-56bc-bce9-c805c73a6765" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186486424 #edb/ref 1074 "First, notice that the position vector of the point $P(4,0,4)$ is $p=⟨4,\\,0,\\,4⟩.$\n\nWe need to find a vector that's perpendicular to the osculating plane. To do that, we use the cross product of the unit tangent $T(-2)$ and the principal normal $N(-2):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}n & =T(-2)\\times N(-2) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}⟨-1,\\,\\sqrt{2},\\,1⟩\\times \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}⟨1,\\,0,\\,1⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{2}}\\cdot \\left|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ -1 & \\sqrt{2} & 1 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}\\right| \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{2}}[\\text{ }\\left|\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{2} & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}\\right|\\,i-\\left|\\begin{aligned}-1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}\\right|\\,j+\\left|\\begin{aligned}-1 & \\sqrt{2} \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{aligned}\\right|\\,k\\text{ }] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{2}}(\\sqrt{2}\\,i+2\\,j-\\sqrt{2}\\,k) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}⟨1,\\sqrt{2},-1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the shortest distance between our plane and the origin is given by\n\n$$\nd=\\frac{|(p-0)\\cdot n|}{‖n‖}=\\frac{|p\\cdot n|}{‖n‖}=|p\\cdot n|,\n$$\n\nsince $‖n‖=1.$ Therefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}d & =|p\\cdot n| \\\\  & =|⟨4,0,4⟩\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}⟨1,\\sqrt{2},-1⟩| \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}|4+0-4| \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186486438 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186486293 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186486438 #edb/ref 1069 "q-127317" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186486438 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186486438 #edb/ref 1071 "Find the shortest distance between the origin and the osculating plane for the curve $r(t)$ at the point where $t=\\pi ,$ given that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(t) & =⟨-3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,4t,\\,3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t⟩ \\\\ T(t) & =\\frac{1}{5}⟨3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,\\,4,\\,3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t⟩ \\\\ N(t) & =⟨\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,0,\\,-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that $T$ and $N$ denote the unit tangent and principal normal, respectively." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186486438 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "c6f5e861-f6b7-50af-904e-74f27b7e3031" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186486438 #edb/ref 1074 "First, notice that the unit tangent and the principal normal at the point where $t=\\pi $ are\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T(\\pi ) & =\\frac{1}{5}⟨3\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\pi ,\\,4,\\,3\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\pi ⟩=\\frac{1}{5}⟨0,4,-3⟩ \\\\ N(\\pi ) & =⟨\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\pi ,\\,0,\\,-\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\pi ⟩=⟨-1,0,0⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAlso, the position vector of the point where $t=\\pi $ is\n\n$$\np=r(\\pi )=⟨-3\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\pi ,\\,4\\pi ,\\,3\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\pi ⟩=⟨3,4\\pi ,0⟩.\n$$\n\nNow, we need to find a vector that's perpendicular to the osculating plane. To do that, we use the cross product of the unit tangent $T(\\pi )$ and the principal normal $N(\\pi ):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}n & =T(\\pi )\\times N(\\pi ) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}⟨0,4,-3⟩\\times ⟨-1,0,0⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}\\cdot \\left|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ 0 & 4 & -3 \\\\ -1 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}\\right| \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}[\\text{ }\\left|\\begin{aligned}4 & -3 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}\\right|\\,i-\\left|\\begin{aligned}0 & -3 \\\\ -1 & 0\\end{aligned}\\right|\\,j+\\left|\\begin{aligned}0 & 4 \\\\ -1 & 0\\end{aligned}\\right|\\,k\\text{ }] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}(3\\,j+4\\,k) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}⟨0,3,4⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the shortest distance between our plane and the origin is given by\n\n$$\nd=\\frac{|(p-0)\\cdot n|}{‖n‖}=\\frac{|p\\cdot n|}{‖n‖}=|p\\cdot n|,\n$$\n\nsince $‖n‖=1.$ Therefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}d & =|p\\cdot n| \\\\  & =|⟨3,4\\pi ,0⟩\\cdot \\frac{1}{5}⟨0,3,4⟩| \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}|0+12\\pi +0| \\\\  & =\\frac{12\\pi }{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186486451 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186486292 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186486451 #edb/ref 1069 "q-33691" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186486451 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186486451 #edb/ref 1071 "Find the Cartesian equation of the osculating plane for a curve $r(t)$ at the point $P(0,1,1)$ where $t=0$, given that\n\n$$\nT(0)=\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3}(\\,i+\\,j+\\,k),\\,N(0)=\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}(\\,i-\\,j).\n$$\n\nNote that $T$ and $N$ denote the unit tangent and principal normal, respectively." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186486451 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "e0dccf1d-9f50-573c-8356-a6a604a3b7cc" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186486451 #edb/ref 1074 "First, notice that the position vector of the point $P(0,1,1)$ is $p=⟨0,1,1⟩.$\n\nTo find the equation of the osculating plane, we need to find a vector that's perpendicular to it. To do that, we find the cross product of the unit tangent $T(0)$ and the principal normal $N(0):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T(0)\\times N(0) & =(\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3}(\\,i+\\,j+\\,k))\\times (\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}(\\,i-\\,j)) \\\\  & =(\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3}\\cdot \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2})\\cdot \\left|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ 1 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & -1 & 0\\end{aligned}\\right| \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{6}}{6}[\\text{ }\\left|\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 0\\end{aligned}\\right|\\,i-\\left|\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{aligned}\\right|\\,j+\\left|\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & -1\\end{aligned}\\right|\\,k\\text{ }] \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{6}}{6}(i+j-2\\,k)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince we are interested only in the direction of the above vector, let's write our perpendicular vector $n$ as \n\n$$\nn=\\sqrt{6}\\cdot (T(0)\\times N(0))=i+j-2\\,k=⟨1,1,-2⟩.\n$$\n\nFinally, let $⟨x,y,z⟩$ denote the position vector of a general point on the osculating plane. Since the plane is perpendicular to $n$ and passes through the tip of the position vector $p$, its Cartesian equation can be written as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(⟨x,y,z⟩-p)\\cdot n & =0 \\\\ ⟨x,y,z⟩\\cdot n-p\\cdot n & =0 \\\\ ⟨x,y,z⟩\\cdot n & =p\\cdot n \\\\ ⟨x,y,z⟩\\cdot ⟨1,1,-2⟩ & =⟨0,1,1⟩\\cdot ⟨1,1,-2⟩ \\\\ 1\\cdot x+1\\cdot y+(-2)\\cdot z & =-1 \\\\ x+y-2z & =-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186486460 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186486342 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186486460 #edb/ref 1069 "q-127137" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186486460 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186486460 #edb/ref 1071 "Find a parametric equation of the osculating plane for the curve  $r(t)$ at the point where $t=0,$ given that\n\n$$\nr(t)=⟨{t}^{3},\\,2-{t}^{2},\\,t⟩,\\,T(0)=⟨0,0,1⟩,\\,N(0)=⟨0,-1,0⟩.\n$$\n\nNote that $T$ and $N$ denote the unit tangent and principal normal, respectively." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186486460 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "f746d7b9-67e2-5aa7-b32a-b632bdb15929" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186486460 #edb/ref 1074 "The osculating plane passes through the point $P$, which is at the tip of the vector $r(0).$ The vectors $T(0)$ and $N(0)$ are parallel to this plane. Therefore, a parametric equation for the plane can be written as\n\n$$\nR(s,u)=r(0)+s\\,T(0)+u\\,N(0),\\,s,u\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).\n$$\n\n \n\nWe have that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(0) & =⟨{0}^{3},\\,2-{0}^{2},\\,0⟩=⟨0,2,0⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, a parametric equation for the osculating plane at the point $(0,2,0)$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}R(s,u) & =r(0)+s\\,T(0)+u\\,N(0) \\\\  & =⟨0,2,0⟩+s⟨0,0,1⟩+u⟨0,-1,0⟩ \\\\  & =⟨0,\\,2-u,\\,s⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417]}
